/* ---- part 2: 1.8 Network topology: graphs, tie-sets and cut-sets ---- */
{s:2,k:'part',n:2,term:"Part 2",say:'Part two. Network topology: forget what each branch holds, keep only its shape, and the equations write themselves.',cues:{p:'Part two',o:'Network topology'},pts:["Graph, tree and co-tree", "Tie-sets: one link, one loop", "Cut-sets: one twig, one split"]},
{s:2,k:'bookfig',term:"Figure: the bridge as a graph",title:"The bridge as a graph: tree and chords",view:[20,40,420,300],say:`Here is a bridge circuit as a graph: four nodes and six branches. Like a metro map, it keeps only who joins whom. The solid tree, four, five and six, reaches every node with no loop. The dashed chords are one, two and three.`,cues:{s1:'Here is a bridge',s2:'The solid tree',cap:'The solid tree',s3:'The dashed chords'},steps:3,cap:'Tree: 4, 5, 6 (solid). Chords: 1, 2, 3 (dashed).',fig:CT_FIG3},
{s:2,k:'bookfig',term:"Figure: tie-sets and cut-sets",title:"One tie-set per chord, one cut-set per twig",view:[0,36,720,300],say:`Each chord closes one loop, its tie-set. Chord one, with twigs four and six, makes the loop [A](/ˈA/), B, D. Cutting a twig isolates one node, its cut-set: twig four, with one and three, cuts B away.`,cues:{s1:'Each chord closes',s2:'Chord one, with',s3:'Cutting a twig',cap:'twig four, with'},steps:3,cap:'One chord, one loop (KVL); one twig, one cut-set (KCL).',fig:CT_FIG4},
{s:2,k:'notebook',term:'Key ideas',title:'Graph, tree, loops and cuts',say:`Every element becomes a branch between two nodes. A tree touches all n nodes with no loop, so it has n minus one twigs. Each link closes one loop: b minus n plus one tie-sets, for ${P('KVL')}. And cutting a twig splits the graph in two: n minus one cut-sets, for ${P('KCL')}.`,cues:{i1:'Every element becomes',i2:'A tree touches',i3:'Each link closes',i4:'And cutting a twig'},items:[['Graph: each element becomes a branch','i1'],['Tree: all n nodes, no loop; n − 1 twigs','i2'],['Each link closes one loop: b − n + 1 tie-sets (KVL)','i3'],['Cutting a twig splits it in two: n − 1 cut-sets (KCL)','i4']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'i1'),CT_box(14,10,612,120)+CT_T(34,44,'1 · element → branch',{fs:24,fw:700})+CT_dot(60,88)+CT_res(60,250,88,ORG)+CT_dot(250,88)+CT_arrow(282,88,352,88,CT_INK,4)+CT_dot(390,88)+CT_L(390,88,580,88,CT_INK,5)+CT_dot(580,88))}
${g('fade',tk(c,['i1',1.6]),CT_gr(340,400,170,{1:'grey',2:'grey',3:'grey',4:'grey',5:'grey',6:'grey'},{lab:1,w:5}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'i2'),CT_gr(340,400,170,{4:'tree',5:'tree',6:'tree'},{w:7,nodes:false})+CT_gr(340,400,170,{})+CT_T(620,186,'twigs 4, 5, 6: n − 1 = 3',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'end',fill:BLU}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'i3'),CT_gr(340,400,170,{1:'link'},{w:6,nodes:false})+CT_gr(340,400,170,{1:'hl',4:'hl',6:'hl'},{w:7,nodes:false})+CT_gr(340,400,170,{})+CT_T(20,640,'link 1 + twigs 4, 6: one loop',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:ORG}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'i4'),`<circle cx="170" cy="400" r="52" fill="none" stroke="${MAG}" stroke-width="4" stroke-dasharray="10 7"/>`+CT_T(20,674,'cut twig 4: {4, 1, 3} isolates B',{fs:22,fw:800,fill:MAG}))}`},
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 3",title:"Worked example 3",say:'Example three: the bridge, tree four, five and six. Link currents act as loop currents, so each twig carries a sum. Tie-set one gives twenty-two I one plus two I three equals thirty-six. Tie-set two gives twenty-eight I two minus eight I three equals thirty-six.',cues:{fk:'Example three',k1:'Link currents act',k2:'Tie-set one',k2b:'gives twenty-two',k3:'Tie-set two',k3b:'gives twenty-eight'},q:CT_Q3,f:CT_F3,hint:'Orient each loop like its link; v = Ri along the arrow.',fk:'fk',steps:CT_S3,live:[0,1,2],rh:76},
{s:2,k:'calc',term:"Worked example 3 (solve)",title:"Worked example 3",say:'Tie-set three runs against branch five: two I one minus eight I two plus twenty I three equals zero. Solve: I three is zero point four amps, then I one is one point six, and I two is one point four. Every cut-set sums to zero, so it checks.',pause:.8,cues:{fk:'Tie-set three',k4:'Tie-set three',k4b:'two I one',k5:'Solve',k5b:'then I one',k6:'Every cut-set',k6b:'so it checks',a:'so it checks'},q:CT_Q3,f:CT_F3,hint:'Orient each loop like its link; v = Ri along the arrow.',fk:'fk',steps:CT_S3,live:[3,4,5],rh:76,ans:['i₁ to i₆: 1.6, 1.4, 0.4, 2, 1 and 3 A','a']},
{s:2,k:'trap2',term:'Exam trap',title:'Exam trap!',say:'The trap: the counts. With four nodes and six branches, students write four twigs and two links. Write n minus one, three twigs, and b minus n plus one, three links.',cues:{x:'The trap: the counts',w:'With four nodes',r:'Write n minus'},wl:"Don't write",rl:'Write',wrong:['n = 4, b = 6:','twigs = n = 4,  links = b − n = 2'],right:['twigs = n − 1 = 3,','links = b − n + 1 = 3'],wv:(c,u,b)=>`
${CT_gr(330,165,115,{1:'bad',2:'bad',4:'bad',5:'bad',3:'link',6:'link'},{w:6})}
${CT_T(20,44,'"4 twigs"',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:TRED})}${CT_T(620,316,'they close a loop',{fs:22,fw:800,a:'end',fill:TRED})}
${CT_L(40,30,600,310,TRED,10,'stroke-opacity="'+TOP+'"')}`,rv:(c,u,b)=>`
${CT_gr(330,165,115,{4:'tree',5:'tree',6:'tree',1:'link',2:'link',3:'link'},{w:6})}
${CT_T(20,44,'3 twigs = n − 1',{fs:24,fw:800,fill:BLU})}${CT_T(620,316,'3 links = b − n + 1',{fs:24,fw:800,a:'end',fill:GRN})}`},
/* ---- recap ---- */
{s:RC,k:'recap',term:'Recap',say:`Recap in three. A practical source swaps forms with the same resistor, the arrow towards the old plus. A tree has n minus one twigs, and b minus n plus one links. Each link gives one tie-set for ${P('KVL')}, and each twig one cut-set for ${P('KCL')}.`,cues:{r1:'A practical source',r2:'A tree has n',r3:'Each link gives'},panels:[["Same terminals","BLU",'ctSwap',['Iₛ = Vₛ / R, the same R,','arrow to the old +.'],'r1'],["Tree and links","ORG",'ctTree',['n − 1 twigs,','b − n + 1 links.'],'r2'],["Loops and cuts","GRN",'ctCut',['Each link: a tie-set, KVL.','Each twig: a cut-set, KCL.'],'r3']],live:[0,1,2]},
/* ---- exam practice ---- */
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why are there n − 1 fundamental cut-sets?",say:'Question one. Why are there n minus one fundamental cut-sets?',cues:{k:'Question one',q:'Why are there'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 1',lv:'U',n:1,q:"Why are there n − 1 fundamental cut-sets?",say:'Count the twigs: a tree has n minus one of them. Removing each twig splits the tree in two, and that defines one cut-set. So there is one fundamental cut-set per twig.',cues:{a1:'Count the twigs',a2:'Removing each twig',a3:'So there is'},al:[["The tree has n − 1 twigs.",'a1'],["Removing each twig splits the tree in two, defining one cut-set.",'a2'],["So there is one fundamental cut-set per twig.",'a3']],vis:(c,u,b)=>`
${g('pop',tk(c,'a1'),CT_gr(330,250,150,{4:'tree',5:'tree',6:'tree'},{w:7,lab:1})+CT_T(310,46,'n = 4 nodes: 3 twigs',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:BLU}))}
${g('pop',tk(c,'a2'),CT_L(210.5,324.5,254.5,280.5,MAG,6)+CT_L(405.5,280.5,449.5,324.5,MAG,6)+CT_L(122,309,156,281,MAG,6))}
${g('rise',tk(c,'a3'),CT_T(310,470,'3 cuts: 3 fundamental cut-sets',{fs:26,fw:800,a:'middle',fill:MAG}))}`},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"Convert 12 V in series with 4 Ω, and 4 A in parallel with 2 Ω.",say:'Question two. Convert twelve volts in series with four ohms, and four amps in parallel with two ohms.',cues:{k:'Question two',q:'Convert twelve volts'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 2',lv:'A',n:2,q:"Convert 12 V in series with 4 Ω, and 4 A in parallel with 2 Ω.",say:'Twelve over four: three amps, in parallel with four ohms, the arrow towards the old plus. Four times two: eight volts, in series with two ohms, plus on the side the arrow pointed to.',cues:{fk:'Twelve over four',k1:'Twelve over four',k1b:'three amps, in',k2:'Four times two',k2b:'eight volts, in'},calc:{f:['Iₛ = Vₛ / R','Vₛ = Iₛ R'],hint:'R keeps its value; only its place changes.',fk:'fk',steps:[["12 V with 4 Ω",["Iₛ = 12 / 4","3 A in parallel with 4 Ω, arrow to the old +"],["k1","k1b"]],["4 A with 2 Ω",["Vₛ = 4 × 2","8 V in series with 2 Ω, + where the arrow pointed"],["k2","k2b"]]],live:[0,1]}},
{s:QZ,k:'pq',term:'Question 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"Current sources of 3 A and 2 A, with 6 Ω and 3 Ω in parallel, feed the same node pair. Find the merged source if the arrows agree, and if they oppose.",say:'Question three. Current sources of three and two amps, with six and three ohms in parallel, feed one node pair. Merge them with the arrows agreeing, then opposing.',cues:{k:'Question three',q:'Current sources of'},pause:3.4},
{s:QZ,k:'pa',term:'Answer 3',lv:'N',n:3,q:"Current sources of 3 A and 2 A, with 6 Ω and 3 Ω in parallel, feed the same node pair. Find the merged source if the arrows agree, and if they oppose.",say:'Agreeing, three plus two makes five amps. Opposing, three minus two leaves one amp, the three amp source\'s way. Either way, six and three in parallel give two ohms. Voltage forms: ten volts or two volts, with two ohms in series.',cues:{fk:'Agreeing, three',k1:'Agreeing, three',k1b:'makes five amps',k2:'Opposing, three',k2b:'leaves one amp',k3:'Either way',k3b:'give two ohms',k4:'Voltage forms',k4b:'ten volts or'},calc:{f:['parallel current sources add with their signs','R = R₁R₂ / (R₁ + R₂)'],hint:'Opposite arrows subtract.',fk:'fk',steps:[["Arrows agree",["3 + 2","5 A"],["k1","k1b"]],["Arrows oppose",["3 − 2","1 A, the way of the 3 A source"],["k2","k2b"]],["Resistance",["6 × 3 / (6 + 3)","R = 2 Ω either way"],["k3","k3b"]],["Voltage forms",["5 × 2 = 10 V;  1 × 2 = 2 V","10 V or 2 V, in series with 2 Ω"],["k4","k4b"]]],live:[0,1,2,3]}},
{s:LS,k:'figs',term:'Your notes',title:'For your notes',say:'Your free notes are the PDF linked in the description. Redraw the two-source merge and the bridge graph, then list its tie-sets and cut-sets.',cues:{t:'Your free notes',f:'Redraw the two'},tags:[["Free notes for this lesson (PDF)", "link in the description"], ["In this lesson", "Source transformation; Network topology: graphs, tie-sets and cut-sets"], ["Key terms and glossary", "at the end of the notes"]],figs:[["Two sources, one load", "12 V with 3 Ω and 18 V with 6 Ω become 4 A and 3 A, merge into 7 A with 2 Ω, then 14 V with 2 Ω: 3.5 A at 7 V."], ["The bridge as a graph", "Four nodes, six branches; tree 4, 5, 6 (solid), chords 1, 2, 3 (dashed); one tie-set per chord, one cut-set per twig."]],note:"Sources your free notes draw on: Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku (Fundamentals of Electric Circuits); William H. Hayt, Jack E. Kemmerly and Steven M. Durbin (Engineering Circuit Analysis); M. E. Van Valkenburg (Network Analysis)"},
{s:OUT,k:'outro',term:'Outro',say:'So remember: same resistor, arrow to the old plus. A tree has n minus one twigs. And one link closes one loop. Next time, mesh analysis and the supermesh. See you there!',cues:{f:'So remember',w1:'same resistor',w2:'A tree has',w3:'And one link',n:'Next time',s:'See you there'},words:[['SAME R, ARROW TO +','BLU','w1'],['n − 1 TWIGS','ORG','w2'],['ONE LINK, ONE LOOP','GRN','w3']],next:["UP NEXT", "Next topic", "Mesh analysis and supermesh", "book"],end:'See you there!'}
